      "path": ".claude/hooks/guard-shell-writes.sh",
      "content": "Relative --git-dir/--work-tree values are resolved too early when a later -C changes Git's directory. Git retains these option values and interprets relative paths against its final directory. For example, from /tmp, `git --git-dir=.git --work-tree=. -C \u003croot\u003e reset --hard` operates on the project, but this hook records both paths under /tmp and allows it. Store the raw option values, then resolve them against the final `here` after parsing all global options (including the equals forms). Add a regression case with -C after these options.",
      "existing_code": "          --git-dir) resolve \"$here\" \"${toks[i+1]:-.}\"; gd=$res; ((i+=2)) ;;\n          --work-tree) resolve \"$here\" \"${toks[i+1]:-.}\"; wt=$res; ((i+=2)) ;;",
      "start_line": 109,
      "end_line": 110,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": ".claude/hooks/guard-shell-writes.sh",
      "content": "The first `cd -` does not necessarily stay in the initial cwd: Bash uses `$OLDPWD`, which may already be set in the shell environment. For example, with cwd `/tmp` and `OLDPWD` pointing at the project, `cd -; git reset --hard` returns to the project, but this initialization keeps both tracked directories at `/tmp` and allows the reset. Treat the initial previous directory as unknown and conservatively handle a HEAD move after returning to it, or obtain the shell's actual previous directory rather than assuming it equals cwd.",
      "existing_code": "prev=$cur # the directory before the last cd; at the start, the cwd (a failed cd - stays put)",
      "start_line": 77,
      "end_line": 77,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
